on the. it's about the first ten years of the Scouting Movement, is it not?

18
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:02,000
I would say that that's a fair assessment. Yes, I took a lot of time to put as much as I could

19
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,000
in the first ten years, the first decade since they were the. that was the most important in terms of

20
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,000
criticality, if you will, the formation of the Boy Scouts of America.

21
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
And the party we're talking about in the title of the Scouting Party is Ernest Thompson, Seaton,

22
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Baden Powell and Dan Beard. This is a study of their relationship as three founders of Scouting.

23
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,000
There are different influences and everything, and it wasn't always an easy relationship between these three, was it?

24
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
No, there are three massive egos and I'm sure that a couple of them wouldn't have made it out of a room

25
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:43,000
if they were all three put together in the same room and started their negotiations of how to make it work.

26
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,000
So it was very rare that all three of them were seen together- once in 1910 and once in 1912,

27
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
but that was by the extent of it. And, yes, photographs were taken, so we do have documentation

28
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
that they were together and they all three survived, but it had to have been quite a meeting of the minds

29
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
when they were together.

30
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,000
What I found really fascinating was- and I think anybody who's been around a little bit in Scouting

31
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:15,000
and has worked in a trooper, a packer, a crew and worked in a district or council level position

32
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:20,000
understands that it, like you said, it takes some massive egos sometimes to make things go

33
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
and they don't always get along, but the product is really what we keep our eyes on.

34
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:31,000
I was fascinated to learn that Ernest Thompson Seaton seems to be the guy with the original idea.

35
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Well, he was, and when he met with Robert Bated-Pole in October of 1906,

36
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:46,000
a BP was finishing off the creation of what he would eventually call his Boy Scouts,

37
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
but he didn't have all of the framework set down.

38
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,000
And when he did speak with Seaton at that meeting on October 30th,

39
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:58,000
the pieces were finally put into place and BP was able to finalize his plan

40
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
in several pamphlets that he published in 1907.

41
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Actually, he published two of the three-

42
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Boy Scouting is a suggestion- and then he talked about Brown Sea and his plan,

43
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
and that's what finally gelled into what we know now as the Boy Scouting movement,

44
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:21,000
which I consider being born, really philosophically born- on October 30th of 1906,

45
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:26,000
but in reality officially born of January 15th 1908,

46
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
dating to the publication of BP's seminal work,

47
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
the first fortnightly issuance of Scouting for Boys.

48
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
The interesting thing is that when Bated-Pole sat down to do this,

49
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
he had already seen Ernest Thompson Seaton's material

50
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
about the Woodcraft Indians.

51
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Well, he had, in fact, when Bated-Pole was-

52
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
and I say Bated-Pole because that's the way he pronounced his name,

53
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:54,000
I know that it's

54
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Oh okay,

55
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Going against the grain, but I have a bad habit of doing that,

56
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,000
But anyway, that being said, BP was vacationing at the house of his publisher

57
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Pearson- I can't recall his first name, Arthur Pearson-

58
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:14,000
That is this house in Surrey, England, in the summer of 1906,

59
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
and this package arrived. this parcel arrived

60
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
and it was shipped by Ernest Seaton to BP

61
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
because Seaton wanted BP's opinion on how to work with him

62
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
in terms of broadcasting his Seaton to Woodcraft Indians.

63
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
The book was the Birchbark role of the Woodcraft Indians

64
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,000
and that would be the book which would help provide the final piece for BP

65
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
to help create his Boy Scouting Scheme, as you call it.

66
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
So that package arrived-

67
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
An unexpected package, mind you, but it arrived nonetheless,

68
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
and that's when the gelling began

69
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:53,000
To kind of fill people in about Ernest Thompson Seaton.

70
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,000
tell us just a little bit about his history.

71
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Well, Ernest Seaton was a Scots-born Canadian

72
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,000
and he came to the United States in the late 1890s

73
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
to eventually get married. essentially is what it was,

74
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:14,000
but he was living, he and his wife, the, her maiden name being Gallatin,

75
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
her name was Grace Gallatin.

76
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,000
They got married and moved to New York in the late 1890s

77
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:24,000
and Seaton published a book of his animal stories

78
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
called Wild Animals I Have Known,

79
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,000
and in that story was, or in that book was, a story

80
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,000
that was based upon his experience with a wolf

81
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:40,000
in the New Mexico lands, a desert out there named Lobo,

82
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:45,000
and the popularity of Lobo's story- The King Wolf of the Karumpaw-

83
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:49,000
that skyrocketed his fame through this book,

84
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
because he'd already recently published the story of Lobo

85
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
in Scribner's Magazine, and so people knew about Lobo

86
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Well, they wanted to hear more about these animal stories

87
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,000
and these animals that he'd come across,

88
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,000
and what he did was he created a genre of a type of story

89
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
to which gave human-like personalities and characteristics

90
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:11,000
to the animals, and so these stories were incredibly popular

91
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
and in fact he made a contract with his publisher, Scribner's,

92
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
that he would take no royalties on the first 2,000 copies

93
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,000
of his book, because they didn't think they would sell.

94
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:22,000
Well, he knew that it would.

95
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:26,000
but for every copy above 2,000, he would take a double royalty

96
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
in a sense, 20%.

97
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Well, after see, that was done around March or April of 1898,

98
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,000
and by December they'd gone through multiple printings

99
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:41,000
and he was up about, in today's dollars, about 4 million bucks.

100
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:46,000
So he was about 200,000 royalties, but about 4 million today.

101
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
so he was quite a wealthy guy.

102
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,000
He could do whatever he wanted to at that point

103
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:53,000
and the scouting thing appealed to him.

104
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:57,000
His approach to nature study was based a lot on

105
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:02,000
Native American spirituality that he had picked up in his travels.

106
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:07,000
Absolutely, He was more concerned with the great spirit

107
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,000
as opposed to classic, traditional Christianity

108
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
and he believed that the Native American-

109
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
or, as he referred to them, of course, the Red Man,

110
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,000
the Native American- was a superior human being

111
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,000
in terms of their spirituality, because they lived off the land

112
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:27,000
and they did not desecrate it, they didn't destroy it,

113
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:30,000
they didn't harm it in so much as they could.

114
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:35,000
so they lived and used the bounty of mother nature

115
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
and they respected her.

116
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:42,000
That was his model for the way an American boy should grow up,

117
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:46,000
being more like in the model of Tecumseh than anyone else.

118
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:50,000
I think that was kind of a minority opinion at the time.

119
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
Well, it would have to have been a minority opinion,

120
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
most definitely. yes,

121
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:58,000
Seedon was. he was a bit of a visionary

122
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:02,000
as compared to his compatriots Powell and Beard.

123
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:08,000
If you look at Baden-Pole and his work on scouting for boys,

124
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,000
he considered the back woodsman

125
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,000
or the knights of the old Chevalric years,

126
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,000
knights of King Arthur, as his model for the scouts.

127
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:21,000
Well, Daniel Carter Beard, Uncle Dan, Dan Beard, as we call him,

128
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:26,000
he looked at as the pioneer, the plainsman,

129
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:31,000
the buckskin man, as the model for the American boy,

130
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
the likes of a Theodore Roosevelt

131
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
and the likes of George Washington

132
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
and the likes of an Abraham Lincoln.

133
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:43,000
Those were his ideas of the model that an American boy should follow.

134
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
And it's interesting to me that the movement that we have today

135
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
comes from these kind of disparate influences

136
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,000
and you can't call one of them right and one of them wrong,

137
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:57,000
but they all came together to form a scouting movement

138
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
that's been pretty durable for the past 100 years.

139
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
Well, if you look at the contributions,

140
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:04,000
you would have to think that Dan Beard's patriotism,

141
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:08,000
that's infused in the Boy Scouts' movement,

142
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:12,000
Baden Pole, looking back to his knights,

143
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:16,000
that was heroics and heroism

144
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,000
That is built in the Boy Scouts.

145
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:23,000
You look at Seton. you look at his vision of the Great Spirit.

146
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
That was just one.

147
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
You look at Christianity and Judaism

148
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:32,000
and you see that multi-religious faiths

149
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:35,000
are built in the Boy Scouts model.

150
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
They're all in there.

151
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:43,000
There's not one particular model that completely dominates,

152
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
Completely dominates.

153
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
VP certainly has the bulk of the system,

154
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
but it doesn't completely dominate.

155
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
You look at the order of the arrow

156
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
that goes back to Seton,

157
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
And the order of the arrow is a huge program

158
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
in the Boy Scouts of America.

159
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
You can't say that one completely dominates,

160
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
but there are certainly major influences

161
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:04,000
by those three individuals.

162
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
You know a lot of histories of scouting

163
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
that I have read over time. take this era

164
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
and they kind of encapsulate it in a few paragraphs.

165
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
While they will mention some of the conflicts

166
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:17,000
and controversies amongst these three men,

167
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:18,000
they kind of downplay it.

168
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
What I really enjoy about your work, David,

169
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:21,000
is you just go straight at it,

170
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
You don't downplay it or anything

171
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
to kind of celebrate the fact that

172
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
they were able to work together.

173
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
It was the fact of what they were able to contribute

174
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
to each other, whether they liked it or not,

175
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
And that's the good thing about it.

176
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,000
The accomplishments speak for themselves.

177
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:39,000
How long do you think you spent working on this thing.

178
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
Well, I tag it to about 10 years,

179
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:42,000
writing about 8 to 10..

180
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
For my lights it was well worth it

181
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
for the product.

182
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
Well, thank you very much.

183
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
I know my wife would like to hear that,

184
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
because there were many a night

185
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:56,000
in the early days of the early 2000s

186
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
to which I was staying up until 2,

187
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
2.30 and 3 in the morning

188
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
with these piles of papers

189
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
at the dinner table in my computer

190
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
And I'm just typing away and thinking

191
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:07,000
this is horrible,

192
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
This is terrible.

193
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
There's nothing going to come out of this

194
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
but besides a big pile of trash,

195
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:14,000
Eventually it turned into something,

196
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,000
so that was a good thing.

197
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,000
There are a couple other major players in here,

198
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
and one would be a familiar name

199
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
to most scouts who've studied a little bit

200
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
about the history of the BSA,

201
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
and that's West.

202
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:30,000
James West was the first executive secretary.

203
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
within a year or so

204
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
He of course made the title of career

205
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
the title of chief scout executive.

206
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
Jim West was

207
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
an incredibly difficult taskmaster

208
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
And if he saw a messy desk

209
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
he would take that cane

210
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
that he walked around with

211
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
and he would sweep that desk clean

212
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,000
and say: go, pick it up, put it in the right spot.

213
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
He would have a good time at my house,

214
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
He would wear his cane out.

215
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
I'm sure he wouldn't be very pleased

216
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
if he saw the 2400 square feet of warehouse

217
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,000
that are loaded up with books over where I write.

218
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:06,000
He comes off as being very business-like

219
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:07,000
and very motivated,

220
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:10,000
but also he was kind of a cold fish, wasn't he?

221
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
Well, he absolutely was.

222
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,000
His biggest goal was to be successful.

223
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
We understand that part.

224
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
But in a personal level

225
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
he wanted to be beloved by the boys,

226
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:25,000
as were Dan Beard and Ernest Seaton.

227
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
But he did not have those skills.

228
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:28,000
He was a cold fish

229
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
and he did not have much of a personality

230
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
other than to make people do exactly what he wanted them to do.

231
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
but he could not get along with the boys

232
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
to the extent that Beard and Seaton were.

233
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
They were the symbols of American scouting

234
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
for those years.

235
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
Seaton fought five and Beard

236
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
for the remaining 25 or so

237
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
while he was alive.

238
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
But that's why he re-received

239
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
a letter of Uncle Dan,

240
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
the beloved uncle of Boy Scouts.

241
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
Just as with the other three,

242
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
without West we don't have scouting as we have it today.

243
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
If you didn't have West,

244
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
you wouldn't have had the last three points of the scout law-

245
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
